  "query_preview": "..Clear skies this morning across southeast Arizona as \ndry westerly flow prevails. Latest WV satellite imagery shows an \nupper low across Nevada that will scoot east across Utah today but \nremain far enough north that it won't have any impact on our \nweather. Another shortwave trough will then drop southeast from the \nPacific NW into the Great Basin Thursday into Friday. This is the \npoint where we start to see some differences within the model \nenvelope. The EPS/ECMWF is more aggressive on the southern and \nwestern extent of jet energy which results in the upper low cutting \noff Friday over Arizona with the GFS/GEFS/GEPS much more progressive \ndepicting an open wave pattern as a benign positively tilted \nshortwave trough moves through. Given the increasing trends of the \npotential for a cut-off low in the past day or so, it's reasonable \nto start introducing PoPs especially later Friday into Saturday and \nthe NBM has done so east of Tucson. If the trends continue with a \ncut-off low, PoPs will need to continue be adjusted higher, though \neven with that said it's not shaping up to be a high impact event. \n\nOnce the aforementioned system moves to the east, dry weather will \nreturn this weekend into early next week. Meanwhile, high \ntemperatures will remain about 3 to 8 degrees below normal through \nthe forecast period.",
